\textbf{Implementation details.}
All experimental pipelines were implemented using the PyTorch framework and executed on a workstation equipped with an NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPU.
The ML baselines utilized \hl{the} scikit-learn library along with their respective official open-source libraries \cite{a1, a2, a3}.
The DL baselines leveraged their official implementations provided by the original authors \cite{a4, a5, a6}.
All DL baselines, including ST-CVAE, were optimized using the AdamW optimizer \cite{x9} for a maximum of 200 epochs with a batch size of 512, employing early stopping with a patience of 20 epochs.
To ensure a strictly fair evaluation of uncertainty quantification, deterministic DL models, i.e., MLP, ResNet, FTT, and TabR, were fundamentally modified.
Their single-node output layers were expanded to simultaneously predict three distinct quantiles (lower, median, and upper), and the conventional MSE loss was replaced by a Pinball loss \cite{x10}.
This architectural adaptation enables the application of \hl{CQR} \cite{x11}.
Conversely, NGBoost, TabPFN, and the proposed ST-CVAE inherently output full probability distributions; thus, CQR was seamlessly applied to their generated predictive distributions without requiring architectural modifications.
Finally, to eliminate hyperparameter bias, the Optuna framework \cite{x12} was utilized to execute 30 trials for every ML and DL baseline, guaranteeing all comparative evaluations were conducted under their optimal configurations.
\hl{To ensure statistical robustness and mitigate overfitting, all performance metrics were evaluated using 15-fold cross-validation.}

\textbf{Comparative performance analysis.}
Table \ref{tab:table2} details the quantitative evaluation results, including prediction accuracy ($R^{2}$-score), Prediction Interval Coverage Probability (PICP) \cite{x13}, and Normalized Mean Prediction Interval Width (NMPIW) \cite{x14}.
Here, PICP and NMPIW are representative measures to evaluate the reliability of uncertainty intervals.
As theorized in Section \ref{subsec:2}, deterministic ML and DL models inherently suffer from mode averaging on multi-modal traffic data, mathematically stalling their $R^{2}$-scores.
The ST-CVAE mitigates this fundamental limitation by learning the unobserved traffic dynamics via the generative latent variables Z, achieving the highest $R^{2}$-score \hl{(0.8188 for dwell time)} and decisively outperforming state-of-the-art models.
Furthermore, assessing the UQ calibration, the ST-CVAE perfectly satisfies the nominal 90\% confidence target with a PICP of \hl{0.8998}, while simultaneously maintaining an extremely tight NMPIW of \hl{0.0608}.
In CIoV precaching operations, this indicates that our model provides mathematically guaranteed safety bounds without excessively widening the predictive intervals, directly enabling the RSU to minimize wasted backhaul bandwidth.

\begin{figure*}[htp] \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{./graph/Graph1.png}
    \caption{Winkler interval score across different target percentiles for various models: (a) baseline models, and (b) models applying CQR. The y-axis denotes the interval score on a logarithmic scale, where lower values indicate better performance in providing narrow and accurate prediction intervals.} \label{fig:graph1}
\end{figure*}

\textbf{Ablation study.}
To isolate the impact of our specific architectural innovations, Table \ref{tab:table3} details a structural ablation study.
The empirical results directly validate the design rationale proposed in Section \ref{subsec:2}.
Replacing the ReGLU-augmented ResBlock backbone with a generic MLP triggers a substantial degradation in the $R^{2}$-score (from 0.8188 to 0.7402).
This proves that explicitly formulating multiplicative feature interactions is strictly necessary for decoding heterogeneous tabular features.
Additionally, the removal of cyclical encoding significantly impairs predictive performance, confirming that preserving the topological continuity of temporal signal phases is critical for spatio-temporal regression.
Applying CQR degrades the $R^{2}$-score but fits the PICP accurately to the confidence intervals.

\textbf{Uncertainty quantification.}
Figure \ref{fig:graph1} evaluates the precision of the generated uncertainty intervals using the Winkler Interval Score (IS) \cite{x15}, which simultaneously penalizes non-coverage and excessive interval width.
The ST-CVAE consistently achieves \hl{the} best IS across all target percentiles compared to highly robust probabilistic baselines like NGBoost.
A low IS mathematically implies that the model attains the mandated safety coverage using significantly narrower predictive boundaries.
In practical edge computing, this high-quality uncertainty quantification prevents the RSU from allocating resource-wasting data buffers for safe content transmission.

\textbf{Sensitivity and robustness analysis.}
Figure \ref{fig:graph2} investigates the stability of the ST-CVAE concerning structural and learning parameters via sensitivity heatmaps. The contiguous dark blue regions, which represent low predictive errors, verify the robustness of the proposed framework. The left panel illustrates that once the hidden dimension ($d_{h}$) and the latent dimension ($d_{z}$) exceed a certain threshold, the predictive performance stabilizes significantly. Similarly, the right panel demonstrates a broad optimal area, indicating that predictive accuracy is maintained in a wide range of learning rates ($\eta$) and KL-divergence weights ($\beta$). These results confirm that the optimal performance of the ST-CVAE is not hypersensitive to slight variations in structural configurations or learning constraints. Consequently, this intrinsic robustness suggests that the framework can guarantee reliable performance without requiring extensive and computationally expensive hyperparameter optimization.

\begin{figure}[ht] \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{./graph/Graph2.png}
    \caption{Sensitivity analysis heatmaps evaluating the impact of structural parameters (hidden dimension $d_{h}$ and latent dimension $d_{z}$) and learning parameters (learning rate $\eta$ and KL-divergence weight $\beta$) on the predictive performance of the ST-CVAE.} \label{fig:graph2}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Computational complexity analysis.}
\hl{In practical CIoV environments, models must operate on resource-constrained RSUs without introducing severe processing delays. To validate this, we conducted a computational overhead analysis, detailed in Table} \ref{tab:table4}. \hl{The experiment was executed on an NVIDIA GTX 1660 equipped with 8GB VRAM, serving as a proxy for an edge computing node. The results demonstrate that while extremely large baseline models like TabPFN demand over 1.5GB of VRAM, the ST-CVAE maintains a remarkably lightweight GPU memory footprint of 76.00MB. Furthermore, it achieves a rapid GPU inference time of 7.91ms. This confirms that the ST-CVAE operates reasonably under limited edge computing resources, satisfying the strict latency requirements of real-time precaching.}

